<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Claudius Ptolemy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 100–c. 170 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> Ptolemy was a Greek mathematician, astronomer and geographer who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in the second century CE. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almagest">Almagest</a> and Geography were the leading authorities in their fields for over a millennium. His works were translated into Arabic under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, preserving them for the medieval world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/claudius-ptolemy.jpg" alt="Claudius Ptolemy" width="286" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Justus van Gent / Pedro Berruguete · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemy_1476_with_armillary_sphere_model.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ptolemy's date and place of birth are both unknown. A 14th-century astronomer, Theodore Meliteniotes, gave his birthplace as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemais-hermiou">Ptolemais Hermiou</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebaid">Thebaid</a>, but the testimony is late and nothing supports it. He lived in or around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and died there. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> is generally taken to mean that he was a Roman citizen. He wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a> and used Babylonian observations and lunar theory. Later Arabic sources often called him the Upper Egyptian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Astronomy took most of his effort. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almagest">Almagest</a>, originally the Mathematical Treatise, is the only surviving comprehensive ancient treatise on astronomy. Following Hipparchus, Ptolemy derived geometrical models for the Sun, Moon and planets from observations, and supplied tables for computing their positions. It also contains a star catalogue with forty-eight constellations. The planetary models were geocentric and almost universally accepted until heliocentric models reappeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution</a>. Robert R. Newton argued in 1977 that Ptolemy fabricated data, a charge that Owen Gingerich and others rejected.</p><p>His Geography is a handbook on drawing maps from coordinates, based on earlier work by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinus-of-tyre">Marinus of Tyre</a>. Its second part catalogues some 8,000 localities, of which about 6,300 have coordinates. He also wrote the Tetrabiblos, an astrological treatise, and the Harmonics, on the mathematics of musical scales.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>For over a thousand years the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almagest">Almagest</a> was the authoritative text on astronomy across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. It was preserved in Arabic manuscripts and translated twice into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in the 12th century, once in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and once in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Plato of Tivoli first translated the Tetrabiblos from Arabic into Latin in 1138. Many abridged introductions to his astronomy circulated among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, which suggests that few truly mastered the mathematics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ptolemy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The House of Wisdom (Bayt al-Hikma), traditionally associated with Harun al-Rashid and reaching its zenith under al-Ma'mun, sponsored the systematic translation of Greek, Persian, Indian and Syriac texts into Arabic; the project preserved Aristotle, Euclid, Galen and <b>Claudius Ptolemy</b> for the medieval world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Claudius Ptolemy</b> wrote the Almagest and the Geography there in the second century, Plotinus studied under the philosopher Ammonius Saccas before founding Neoplatonism in Rome, and Hero of Alexandria described machines and devices.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almagest of <b>Claudius Ptolemy</b>, written in the 2nd century CE, gave astronomers a geometrical model of the planets, built of circles upon circles, that predicted their positions well, and the writings of Galen governed anatomy and medicine.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemais-hermiou">Ptolemais Hermiou</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.48,31.8&amp;year=145" aria-label="Ptolemais Hermiou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5389 1191 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Claudius Ptolemy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5839 1461h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5839 1461h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.48,31.8&amp;year=145">What was here in 145 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
